WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.910
get to audience questions.

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:06.160
I love this first one because it's something
I've thought about for a very, very long time.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.110
Really, since I first became engaged in politics
when I was in high school.

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:11.400
The question is, Hey David, how could anyone,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.930
it will be undecided about whether or not
they're going to vote for Donald Trump.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:21.570
I also want to know how this is possible and
it seems to me that there's a few different

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:22.570
things going on.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:27.340
I mean, I actually want to know how is it
possible to really be a swing voter?

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:33.200
Do they not know or pay attention to what
the parties and candidates represent?

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.199
Are they voting based on personalities or
physical appearance or something else?

00:00:38.199 --> 00:00:42.589
It is never really made sense to me and I
think that there's a couple of different things

00:00:42.589 --> 00:00:43.589
going on.

00:00:43.589 --> 00:00:49.159
First of all, a lot of these swing voters
are uninformed and I, I'm not saying this

00:00:49.159 --> 00:00:56.239
as a pejorative, we've seen, we've analyzed
in fact a lot of interviews with voters who,

00:00:56.239 --> 00:01:02.059
one was with a group of women who said, I
voted Obama in 2012 and then I voted Trump

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:04.360
in 2016 they were clueless.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.720
I mean they didn't know anything about what
was going on.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.470
So I think part of it is that they are unaware
of the facts and probably making decisions

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:18.480
on the basis of rhetoric that they are not
then researching the truth of a, they are

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.040
falling for the talking points of one versus
the other.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.800
I don't think it's particularly good for our
society.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:29.540
I mean, listen, I'd rather between Obama and
Trump, I'm voting for Obama as a society.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.620
I don't know that we're better off for people
falling for either candidates or either parties

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:37.410
talking points in terms of what it means about
critical thinking and the ability to analyze

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.170
assertions for yourself.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:43.100
But there, there is that element, first and
foremost, people who just don't really know

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.380
what's going on.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:49.420
Um, there's another thing though that I think
needs to be mentioned when we talk about,

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:55.840
uh, the undecideds and the swing voters and
the independence, which is there is a sort

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:02.950
of psychological pleasure that some people
get by being the enlightened centrist.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:09.060
And you see there are actually some, uh, media
people who have YouTube channels or whatever

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:14.200
based on the idea that they are independent
and they are centrists and they will sometimes

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.260
vote for Republicans and sometimes vote for
Democrats.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:24.510
Is it possible that someone's ideology might
sometimes point to voting for a Republican

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.250
and other times point to voting for a Democrat?

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:28.329
Absolutely.

00:02:28.329 --> 00:02:30.939
It's more common in very local races.

00:02:30.939 --> 00:02:32.180
It's much less common.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:38.510
For example, at the Senate level, I'm sure
we can find examples, uh, where you have,

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:44.019
you know, a very, uh, a Democrat that's conservative
on issues you disagree with them on.

00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:48.121
And a Republican who is more liberal, for
example, on issues where you agree, so you

00:02:48.121 --> 00:02:49.999
might vote for the Republican.

00:02:49.999 --> 00:02:51.819
These are very rare cases.

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:58.370
So I've met a lot of people who get some kind
of a satisfaction from sort of feeling as

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:04.590
though they are above the peons who are on
the left or on the right, Democrats or Republicans.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:10.159
But I actually think it's often they who are
the ignorant ones because they seem to, uh,

00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:18.900
uh, they, they seem to interpret centrism
as being more discerning and more fact based

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:23.760
when it comes to deciding who to vote for
when in reality they're missing that the differences

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.389
between Republicans and Democrats left and
right.

00:03:26.389 --> 00:03:31.779
The differences are so obvious that to be
the enlightened centrist is the furthest thing

00:03:31.779 --> 00:03:34.379
from enlightened that you could possibly be.

00:03:34.379 --> 00:03:38.139
So I think that that's also a part of it is
the combination of some people who are uninformed,

00:03:38.139 --> 00:03:41.859
some people who like the idea of just being
contrarian and saying, I'm, I'm a different

00:03:41.859 --> 00:03:42.859
thing.

00:03:42.859 --> 00:03:43.859
I'm not like you guys.

00:03:43.859 --> 00:03:44.859
And I'm not like you guys.

00:03:44.859 --> 00:03:45.859
I'm just the, I'm the centrist.

00:03:45.859 --> 00:03:46.859
I sometimes about this way.

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:50.959
Sometimes the other way, everyone that I've
personally talked to, and this is merely anecdotal,

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:54.980
when you ask them about their politics, their
politics actually make it very clear.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:59.199
You should always voting for a Democrat or
you should always have been voting for a Republican.

00:03:59.199 --> 00:04:00.870
You just don't seem to realize it for yourself.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:08.349
Uh, it's, it's a sort of a sad commentary
about education, um, in the United States.

00:04:08.349 --> 00:04:12.150
You then also have voters who are just not
very politically engaged and they like to

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:15.639
say, I vote for whoever is best in a particular
election.

00:04:15.639 --> 00:04:17.860
And very often that has nothing to do with
policy at all.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:24.600
It actually is personality and um, the way
that the person campaigns and sort of a feeling

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:29.640
that someone gets judged that as you will
in terms of whether that's a good thing or

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:30.640
not a good thing.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:36.980
But yeah, I also don't really understand,
I mean, listen, if today if someone is unsure

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:40.770
whether they are voting for a Democrat or
Trump, I want to hear from you.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:45.250
Even the furthest right Democrat, which I
guess would be who's furthest, right?

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:49.070
I mean, is it like a Biden of the people that
are still in it?

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:52.440
Biden followed by Buddha judge, I guess of
the people that are, that are still in the

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.330
running or the furthest right.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.980
Of course, I'm voting for them over Donald
Trump.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:01.290
And I would be very interested to hear during
live calls from someone who might vote for

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:02.980
Trump or might vote for a Democrat.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:06.539
I want to hear on what issue is it that is
keeping you from deciding

